silience: B handles stress well. C keeps calm in crisis. D responds adequately. A unknown.\n\nTechnical competence: A sees complex connections, spatial vision. B good weather assessment, computer skills. C correct decisions quickly, understands complicated technology? Not explicitly but decision quality. D solves problems extremely well, concentration.\n\nCrew cooperation: B uncooperative, nasty remarks. C positive atmosphere, puts concern for others, but also egocentric and communication difficulty. D know-it-all, moody. A show-off, unfriendly.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously. A leadership qualities. B conscientious. C puts concern for others.\n\nCommunication: B nasty remarks. C difficulty communicating ideas. D know-it-all. A unfriendly.\n\nOverall, B has strong reliability and stress, but crew cooperation concerns. C has strong crew cooperation (positive atmosphere) and stress resilience, decision quality, but communication and egocentric issues. D has responsibility and problem solving, but attitude issues. A has leadership and anticipation but attitude issues.\n\nWhich is best? The role is safety-critical; crew cooperation is essential. B's uncooperative behavior is a serious red flag. C's egocentric and communication difficulty also concerning, but positive atmosphere may mitigate. D's moody and know-it-all also concerning. A's unfriendly also concerning.\n\nThus C might be the best despite communication issues, because positive atmosphere and calmness are crucial. However, C's lack of willingness to further education may affect adaptability. But maybe still okay.\n\nWe could argue that C's strengths outweigh B's crew cooperation concerns, and that C's calmness and decision quality are vital. So we could vote for C.\n\nAlternatively, we could propose that D's responsibility and problem solving, plus adequate stress handling, make D a strong candidate, but his moody attitude may be a problem.\n\nGiven the panel currently split between B and